    'article' => [
        'intro' => <<<'TXT'
        New Jersey sales tax takes Purchase amount and ZIP code and returns Sales tax. Nothing is looked up on this page: sales tax is computed from purchase amount and the other boxes, so an unusual pair of values gives an honest answer rather than an error.
        TXT,
        'sections' => [
            ['title' => 'How the New Jersey sales tax calculator works', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <p>New Jersey sales tax answers one question from 2 numbers, and the answer is Sales tax.</p><p>The relationship it solves reads Sales tax = get_sales_tax(Purchase amount, ZIP code).</p><p>The result panel carries Sales tax, each in a box of its own rather than buried in a sentence.</p>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'What to enter, and what comes back', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <p>There are 2 boxes to fill in; every one of them is used by the formula.</p><ul><li><strong>Purchase amount</strong> — a plain number, no unit</li><li><strong>ZIP code</strong> — a plain number, no unit. New Jersey ZIP codes are mostly 4 digits beginning with a 7 or 8.&amp;nbsp;</li></ul><p>What you get back is Sales tax, shown next to the form rather than on a page of its own.</p>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'Reading the answer', 'html' => '<p>None of the boxes on this page carries a unit chip: Sales tax takes a plain number and the answer inherits whatever scale the inputs were measured in.</p>'],
            ['title' => 'The formula, and what turns it round', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <p>Below is the arithmetic in full. Every symbol on the right of an equals sign is one of the boxes on the form.</p><ul><li><strong>Sales tax</strong> = <code>get_sales_tax(Purchase amount, ZIP code)</code></li></ul>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'Checking an answer by hand', 'html' => '<p>A result here can always be checked: enter it in its own box, leave a different one empty, and the page should hand the first value back.</p>'],
            ['title' => 'Where the numbers usually go wrong', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <ul><li><strong>The answer typed back in.</strong> A result box can be typed into, and doing so changes the question rather than the arithmetic: with Sales tax is a result filled in, the page solves for a different variable instead. Check which box is blank before reading the panel.</li><li><strong>Purchase amount left as a guess.</strong> A figure entered here because the box had to have something in it is the single most common way a correct formula produces a wrong answer. If Purchase amount is unknown, solve for it instead of estimating it — leave it blank.</li><li><strong>ZIP code left as a guess.</strong> A figure entered here because the box had to have something in it is the single most common way a correct formula produces a wrong answer. If ZIP code is unknown, solve for it instead of estimating it — leave it blank.</li><li><strong>Measurements mixed across scales.</strong> Entering a rounded value is fine; rounding twice is not. Keep full precision in Purchase amount and let the panel round the answer once.</li></ul>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'What each box means', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <p>The boxes on the New Jersey sales tax form, one at a time: what each one wants, how it is measured, and what the page does if you leave it empty.</p><ul><li><strong>Purchase amount</strong> takes a plain number with no unit attached. It feeds Sales tax. Fill it from a measurement rather than a memory — Purchase amount is the kind of value that gets estimated once and then copied from page to page for years.</li><li><strong>ZIP code</strong> takes a plain number with no unit attached. New Jersey ZIP codes are mostly 4 digits beginning with a 7 or 8.&amp;nbsp;. It feeds Sales tax. Fill it from a measurement rather than a memory — ZIP code is the kind of value that gets estimated once and then copied from page to page for years.</li></ul><p>Every one of those boxes is optional in a sense: fill all but one and the page solves for the one you left. It is only when two are blank that the page has nothing to choose between.</p>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'What each result tells you', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <p>Each box below is a different question about the same calculation. Read them in this order:</p><ul><li><strong>Sales tax</strong> is a plain number. It is worked out from Purchase amount and ZIP code. Compare it with the defaults above before trusting it. A figure far from that range is worth checking against a hand calculation. It is not stored, logged or carried to the next page: change a box and you get a new number, close the tab and it is gone.</li></ul>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'Getting an answer, step by step', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <ol><li><strong>Collect the inputs.</strong> Have Purchase amount and ZIP code ready and all in one unit. The page will convert whatever a chip says it is; it cannot tell that one of the numbers was measured in something else.</li><li><strong>Enter them and press Calculate.</strong> Fill the form — starting with Purchase amount — and press Calculate. The panel updates in place, and the fields you typed into keep your numbers.</li><li><strong>Read the answer against the defaults.</strong> Check Sales tax against the worked example above. If the two disagree by a round factor, the chip or the decimal separator is the place to look first.</li><li><strong>Change one box to check your understanding.</strong> Double one input, leaving the rest alone, and press Calculate again. The direction the answer moves tells you whether that box sits in the numerator or the denominator — and whether the number you got a moment ago was plausible.</li></ol><p>That is the whole procedure. Everything else on this page is the reasoning behind it.</p>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'Limits worth knowing', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <ul><li><strong>It scales in a straight line.</strong> Sales tax follows from a proportional relationship, so a percentage error in one input passes through as roughly the same percentage error in the answer.</li><li><strong>It is a formula, not a model of your case.</strong> The page assumes Purchase amount and ZIP code are measured, independent and in the unit the chip says. It has no way to know that one of them was estimated, that two were taken at different times of day, or that the relationship shifts at the extremes of its range.</li></ul>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'Where this calculation comes up', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <p>The typical use is one direction of the relationship: Purchase amount and ZIP code measured, Sales tax wanted.</p><p>Because nothing is stored between visits, the page is safe for one-off sums and for repeated ones — there is no history to clear and no account to keep.</p><p>If the answer you need is a step further on, the nearest pages are <a href="/finance/free-cash-flow-to-equity">Free cash flow to equity (FCFE) Calculator</a>, <a href="/finance/free-cash-flow-to-firm">Free cash flow to firm (FCFF) Calculator</a> and <a href="/finance/free-float">Free float Calculator</a>.</p>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'About this page', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <p>New Jersey sales tax is a calculator, not an article with a form attached: the numbers in the panel come from the formulas on this page, applied to whatever is in the boxes, and nothing is looked up in a table.</p><p>Its own defaults are run through its own formulas every time the page is built, so the examples above are not illustrations: if the relationship changed, the numbers in the article would change with it, and a page whose arithmetic stopped agreeing with its text would fail the check rather than quietly drift.</p><p>What the page keeps is nothing. The calculation is unchanged between visits. has no account, no history and no server-side record of what you typed; the fields hold your numbers for as long as the tab is open, and the share button builds a link from them only if you ask it to.</p><p>It belongs to the same catalogue as the rest of the site, and follows the same rule as all of them: the formula is printed, the working is shown, and the answer can be checked by hand if you want to.</p><p>The formula is printed in full above precisely so that the page can be argued with. Compare it with the reference you are working from; if they differ, the difference will be in a constant or a unit, and both are visible in the text.</p>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'Where this fits among the other calculators', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <p>A criterion for choosing between these pages: New Jersey sales tax answers a different question from the one above, even where the two share a sentence of explanation.</p><p>The nearest are <a href="/finance/free-cash-flow-to-equity">Free cash flow to equity (FCFE) Calculator</a>, <a href="/finance/free-cash-flow-to-firm">Free cash flow to firm (FCFF) Calculator</a> and <a href="/finance/free-float">Free float Calculator</a>. Each takes its own inputs and reports its own answer, and none of them shares a stored value with this page — open two in separate tabs and they will not interfere.</p><p>If the value you need is not the one reported here but a step before or after it, one of those pages probably reports it directly rather than making you convert by hand.</p>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'Putting the answer to work', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <p>An answer from New Jersey sales tax is a number to act on, so the useful question is what to do with Sales tax once the panel shows it.</p><ul><li><strong>Record the inputs with the answer.</strong> A number on its own is not reproducible. Write down Purchase amount and ZIP code alongside it — a week later the answer is checkable and the reasoning is not.</li><li><strong>Re-run it when a measurement changes.</strong> The page keeps nothing between visits, which is a feature: every figure is worked out from the numbers in front of it, so updating one input and pressing Calculate again gives a fresh answer rather than an average of the old and the new.</li><li><strong>Keep the formula with the figure.</strong> A number from a page like this one travels better than a number from a spreadsheet cell, because the formula that produced it is visible on the same page and does not depend on a file being kept.</li><li><strong>Compare rather than convert.</strong> Run it once for the case in front of you and once for a case you understand well. The gap between the two answers tells you more about your own situation than either number does on its own.</li></ul><p>The page is a calculator, not a system of record. Copy what you need out of it, keep it with the inputs that produced it, and come back with different numbers whenever the inputs change.</p>
            TXT],
        ],
        'faq' => [
            ['q' => 'What does the New Jersey sales tax calculator work out?', 'a' => 'Sales tax, from Purchase amount and ZIP code. The relationship is Sales tax = get_sales_tax(Purchase amount, ZIP code).'],
            ['q' => 'Which values do I need before I start?', 'a' => 'Purchase amount, ZIP code. Enter purchase amount and ZIP code; Sales tax is the box to leave alone unless it is the value you came for.'],
            ['q' => 'How precise is the result?', 'a' => <<<'TXT'
            The arithmetic runs at full precision and only the display of Sales tax is rounded. Rounding twice — once by hand, once by the page — is what makes two answers that should match differ in the last digit.
            TXT],
            ['q' => 'What happens if I leave a box empty?', 'a' => 'The page needs every box filled: Sales tax is fixed as the value it works out, so there is nothing to solve for when an input is missing.'],
            ['q' => 'Does it round to the nearest whole number?', 'a' => 'It rounds for display only; the underlying value of Sales tax keeps more digits than the panel prints.'],
        ],
